sombra (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  Everything since 0.1.0. The controls it writes and the machine it writes them
  on are unchanged; what changed is how it is driven and what it records.

  * The daemon is driven over the system bus instead of a unix socket, on
    io.github.svesh87.Sombra. Who may drive it is a bus policy file and one
    polkit action with allow_active=yes, so the person at this screen is
    allowed without being asked anything.
  * The group sombra is no longer needed and no longer created. Nobody has to
    join anything or log back in to use the console. An installation that has
    the group can keep it; it grants nothing.
  * The --socket option became --bus-address on sombrad, sombra and
    sombra-tray.
  * The console no longer polls: it reads what the daemon publishes and is told
    what changes.
  * External monitors are driven over DDC/CI. sombra monitor list says which
    ones answer, sombra monitor level sets one or all of them, and each
    monitor's level goes into every telemetry sample. A write is confirmed by
    reading it back, which is also how a hand on the monitor's own buttons is
    noticed. The internal panel is not among them: a laptop panel does not
    answer DDC/CI.
  * How much telemetry is kept is a setting, retention_days, from one day to
    thirty. The depth is the ring's own size, so changing it rebuilds the file
    and carries the samples across. Recording can be switched off without
    losing what is already recorded.
  * The backlight has a ceiling as well as a floor, and both bind the
    automation and the hand alike.
  * A reference page behind the "?" in the console, in all three languages: it
    describes every control of every tab and opens at the section of the tab
    the button was pressed on.
  * The panel icon says which mode is running, whether the emergency guard is
    clamping and whether the daemon is answering at all. The console's window
    and its entry in a dock now carry the application's own icon.
  * A slider is no longer repainted from a status reply while a hand is on it,
    and a monitor's slider follows the hand instead of jumping when it is let
    go.
  * The depth of the telemetry ring can be changed with the arrows of its field
    again.
  * sombrad --version no longer segfaults. The monitor library starts a thread
    at load time, and a process exiting immediately after loading it killed that
    thread mid-startup.
  * A telemetry ring written in an older layout is converted when the daemon
    opens it; one it cannot read is kept aside rather than replaced silently.

 -- Sergei Sveshnikov <sergei@svesh.dev>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:33:59 +0200

